{"name":"Sandy Ridge","hex":"#a18e77","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sandy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered with sand.","Sprinkled with sand."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sandwich","Ellipsis of pecan sandy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sandy"},{"term":"ridge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The back of any animal; especially the upper or projecting part of the back of a quadruped.","Any extended protuberance; a projecting line or strip."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a ridge.","To extend in ridges."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sandy Ridge, Hong Kong","blocks":[{"text":"Sandy Ridge, is a hill on the east side of Lo Wu in the New Territories, Hong Kong. The village of Sha Ling (沙嶺), is located south of the hill besides Man Kam To Road. Sandy Ridge is completely within the Frontier Closed Area of Hong Kong adjacent to the territory's border with Shenzhen in mainland China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Running_shed_in_lo_wu.jpg/330px-Running_shed_in_lo_wu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Ridge%2C_Hong_Kong"},{"title":"Ridge","blocks":[{"text":"A ridge is a long, narrow, elevated geomorphologic landform, structural feature, or a combination of both separated from the surrounding terrain by steep sides. The sides of a ridge slope away from a narrow top, the crest or ridgecrest, with the terrain dropping down on either side. The crest, if narrow, is also called a ridgeline. Limitations on the dimensions of a ridge are lacking. Its height above the surrounding terrain can vary from less than a meter to hundreds of meters. A ridge can be either depositional, erosional, tectonic, or a combination of these in origin and can consist of either bedrock, loose sediment, lava, or ice depending on its origin. A ridge can occur as either an isolated, independent feature or part of a larger geomorphological and/or structural feature. Frequently, a ridge can be further subdivided into smaller geomorphic or structural elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg/330px-%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg/500px-Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg/500px-Tsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A mountain ridge from Mount Otensho to Mount Tsubakuro in Japan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATsubakurodake_from_Otenshodake_2002-8-22.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/The_Table1.jpg/500px-The_Table1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/The_Table1.jpg/1280px-The_Table1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/The_Table1.jpg/500px-The_Table1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The edges of tuyas can form ridges.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Table1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg/500px-Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg/1280px-Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg/500px-Vihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pirin Mountain main ridge – view from Koncheto knife-edge ridge towards the pyramidal peaks Vihren and Kutelo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVihren_Pirin_IMG_0859.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Bristol_tenn_ridgelines2.jpg/500px-Bristol_tenn_ridgelines2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Bristol_tenn_ridgelines2.jpg/500px-Bristol_tenn_ridgelines2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A strike ridge within the Appalachian Mountains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABristol_tenn_ridgelines2.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sandy":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/En-us-sandy.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/0d/En-us-sandy.ogg/En-us-sandy.ogg.mp3"}],"ridge":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/En-us-ridge.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/09/En-us-ridge.ogg/En-us-ridge.ogg.mp3"}]}}}